WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.160
Let's say you're trying to lose 20 pounds,
or boost your immunity,

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.680
or increase your ability to fight COVID,
or even cancer.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.840
Well, the amazing thing is,
with the right diet,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.040
you're well on your way to
achieving these vital health goals.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.480
Welcome to the Nutrition Facts Podcast.
I'm your host, Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.240
Today it’s all about the gut,
otherwise known as the gastrointestinal tract.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.040
And we’ll start with a study
that looks at the microbiome

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.960
of those eating plant based diets.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.880
Almost 2,500 years ago,
Hippocrates stated that

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.520
“All disease begins in the gut.”

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.120
Feed our gut bacteria right,
with whole plant-foods,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.080
and they feed us right back
with beneficial compounds

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.440
like butyrate, which gut
bugs make from fiber.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.240
Feed them wrong, on the other
hand, and they can produce

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.440
detrimental compounds like
TMAO, which our gut bugs

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.480
make from cheese, seafood,
eggs, and meat.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.200
Now we used to think that
TMAO only contributed to

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.920
cardiovascular diseases, like
heart disease and stroke,

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.200
but more recently it has
been linked to everything from

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.880
psoriatic arthritis to
polycystic ovary syndrome.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.600
I’m most concerned about
our leading killers, though.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.800
If you look at the top ten
causes of death in the United States,

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.640
we know about heart disease
and stroke, killers #1 and #5,

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.600
but recently, an association
has been between blood levels

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.440
of TMAO and the risks of
various cancers, killer #2.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.440
It could be the inflammation
caused by TMAO that explains

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.480
the link between TMAO and
cancer, but it could also be

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.280
oxidative stress such as
free radicals, DNA damage,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.360
or a disruption in protein folding.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.200
Killer #4 are chronic
obstructive pulmonary diseases

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.280
like emphysema, and TMAO is
associated with premature death

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.080
in patients with exacerbated COPD,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.800
though they suspect it’s
just due to them dying

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.360
from more cardiovascular disease.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.960
The link to stroke is a
no-brainer—no pun intended—

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.680
because of the higher
blood pressure associated

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.280
with higher TMAO levels as
well as the greater likelihood

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.560
of clot formation in
those with atrial fibrillation.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.440
And those with higher TMAO
levels also appear to have

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.200
worse strokes and four
times the odds of death.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.160
Killer #6 is Alzheimer’s disease.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.120
Does TMAO even get up into the brain?

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.200
Yes, TMAO is present in
human cerebrospinal fluid,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.160
which bathes the brain, and
indeed the levels are higher

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.120
in both those with mild
cognitive dysfunction

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.840
and those with Alzheimer’s
disease dementia.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.680
In the brain, TMAO has been shown
to induce neuronal senescence

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.520
(meaning deterioration with age),
increased oxidative stress

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.840
and impaired mitochondrial function,
all of which may contribute

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.160
to brain aging and cognitive impairment.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.800
Killer #7 is diabetes, and
people with higher TMAO levels

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.640
are approximately 50% more
likely to have diabetes too.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.000
Killer #8 is pneumonia, and
TMAO predicts fatal outcomes

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.880
in pneumonia patients even
without evident heart disease.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.160
Killer #9 is kidney disease,
and TMAO is strongly related

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.280
to kidney function and predicts
fatal outcomes there as well.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.080
Over a period of five years,
more than a half of chronic kidney

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.800
disease patients who started
out with an average or higher

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.080
TMAO levels were dead,
whereas among those in the

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.000
lowest third of levels,
nearly 90% remain alive.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.520
Okay, so how can we lower the
TMAO levels in our blood?

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.320
Because TMAO originates from
dietary sources, we could limit

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.960
our intake of choline and
carnitine-rich foods,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.160
but they’re "so widespread", we’re
talkin’ meat, eggs, and dairy.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.000
Therefore, restriction of foods
rich in TMAO-creating nutrients

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.880
may not be practical.
I mean, can’t we just get

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.600
a vegan fecal transplant?

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.920
Vegan donors were kind
enough to provide

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.120
the investigators with
a fresh morning sample.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.080
If you remember, if you
give a vegan a steak,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.640
despite all the carnitine in the meat,
they make almost no TMAO

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.760
compared to a meat-eater, presumably
because they haven’t been

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.560
fostering steak-eating
bugs in their gut.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.000
Remarkably, even if you give
plant-based eaters the equivalent

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.440
of a 20-ounce steak
every day for two months,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.040
only about half start ramping
up production of TMAO

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.160
showing just how far their
gut flora had to change.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:29.120
The capacity of veggie feces to
churn out TMAO is almost nonexistent.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.960
So, instead of eating healthier,
why not just get

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.400
some of that sweet vegan
poop off the brown market.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.040
In a double-blind, randomized,
controlled trial,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.360
research subjects either got vegan
poop or their own poop back.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.040
The complete stool production
was stirred—not shaken—

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.880
and then infused through
a hose down their nose,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.520
and . . . it didn’t work.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.200
First of all, the vegans they
recruited for their study

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.440
started out making TMAO themselves,
as opposed to the other study

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.200
where they didn’t make any at all.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.880
This may be because the other
study required the vegans

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.320
to have been plant-based for
at least a year, and this study didn’t.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.760
So yeah, not much of a change in
TMAO running through their bodies

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.360
two weeks after getting the vegan poop,
but the vegan poop they got

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.680
seemed to start out with some capacity
to produce TMAO in the first place.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.760
So, the failure to improve after
the vegan fecal transplant

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.840
could be related to limited
baseline microbiome differences

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.160
as well as the continuation of an
omnivorous diet after the transplant.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.520
What’s the point of trying
to reset your microbiome

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.200
if you’re just going to eat meat?

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.440
Well, the researchers didn’t
want to switch people

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.560
to a plant-based diet since
they knew that alone can change

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.840
your microbiome, and they didn’t
want to introduce any extra factors.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.640
Bottom line—no pun intended—

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.520
it looks like there may
not be any shortcuts.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.040
We may just have to
eat a healthier diet.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.720
Did you know that more
than half of IBS sufferers
(irritable bowel syndrome)

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.720
appear to have a form of atypical food allergy?
Here’s the story.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.120
Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is
a chronic gastrointestinal disorder

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.040
that affects about one in ten.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.720
You may have heard about
those low-FODMAP diets,

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.200
but they don't appear to work any better
than the standard advice to avoid things

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:29.040
like coffee or spicy and fatty foods; in
fact, you can hardly tell which is which.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.520
But most IBS patients do seem to react
to specific foods, such as wheat, dairy,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.280
soy sauce, or eggs, though when
you test them for typical food allergies,

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:41.680
they may come up negative on a skin
prick test. But what you want to know

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.000
is not what happens on their skin but
inside their gut when you eat them.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.520
Enter confocal laser endomicroscopy.
How cool is this?

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.360
You can snake a microscope down
someone's throat into their gut

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.000
and drip on some foods
and watch in real-time

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.560
as the gut wall becomes
inflamed and leaky.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.920
You can actually see the
cracks forming within minutes.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.000
But it had never been
tested on a largeÂ&nbsp;group

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.080
of IBS patients until now.
Using this new technology,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.640
researchers found that more
than half of IBS sufferers have

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.960
this kind of reaction to various foods,
what they call an atypical food allergy,

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.560
that flies under the radar
of traditional allergy tests.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.800
Exclude those foods from the diet,
and you get a significant alleviation

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.960
of symptoms. But outside of
a research setting, there's no way

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.600
to know which foods are the culprit
without trying your own exclusion diet.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.040
And there's no greater exclusion
diet than excluding everything.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.360
A 25-year-old woman had complained of
abdominal pain, bloating, and diarrhea

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.520
for a year, and drugs didn't seem
to help. But after fasting for 10 days,

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.040
her symptoms improved considerably
and appeared to stay that way

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.200
at least 18 months later. And it
wasn't just subjective improvement.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.920
They took biopsies that showed
the inflammation go down,

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.320
directly measured her bowel irritability,
and stuck expanding balloons

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.120
and electrodes in her rectum
to actually measure changes

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.240
in her sensitivity to pressure
and electrical stimulation.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:12.800
Fasting seemed to
kind of reboot her gut,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:17.200
but just because it worked for her
doesn't mean it works for others.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.800
Case reports are most useful when they
inspire researchers to put it to the test.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.360
Despite research efforts, medical
treatment for this condition

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.040
is still unsatisfactory.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.360
We can try to suppress
the symptoms with drugs,

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.000
but what do you do when
even that doesn't work?

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.080
Eighty-four IBS patients, 58 of whom
who failed basic treatment

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.920
which consisted of pharmacotherapy
and brief psychotherapy,

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.080
36 of the 58 who were still suffering
underwent 10 days of fasting,

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.040
whereas the other 22 stuck
with the basic treatment.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.560
And those in the fasting group
experienced significant improvements

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:54.480
in abdominal pain, diarrhea,
loss of appetite, nausea, anxiety,

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.800
and interference with life in general,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.680
which was significantly better
than the control group.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.600
The researchers concluded that
fasting therapy could be useful

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.000
for treating moderate to
severe patients with IBS.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.080
Unfortunately, patient allocation was
neither blinded nor randomized,

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.000
so the comparison to the
control group doesn't mean much.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.280
They were also given IV vitamins---
B1 and vitamin C---

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:22.880
which seems typical of these
Japanese fasting trials,

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:26.720
even though one wouldn't expect to
get vitamin deficiency syndromes---

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.400
beriberi or scurvy---to present
within just 10 days of fasting.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.640
And they were also kept isolated,

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.360
and maybe that made the
psychotherapy work better?

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:40.480
It's hard to tease out just the fasting
effects. Psychotherapy alone can provide

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.440
lasting benefits. One hundred and one
outpatients with irritable bowel

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.960
syndrome were randomized to medical
treatment or medical treatment

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.640
with three months of psychotherapy.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.320
After three months, the psychotherapy
group did better, and the difference

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.720
was even more pronounced a year later---
a year after the psychotherapy ended.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.680
Better at 3 months and
even better at 15 months.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.040
Psychological approaches appear
to work about as well

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.360
as antidepressant drugs for IBS,

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.160
but the placebo response for
IBS is on the order of 40%,

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.280
and that's whether we're talking
about psychological interventions

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:15.440
or drugs or alternative
medicine approaches.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:20.400
So essentially nothing---a sugar pill---
improves symptoms 40% of the time.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:28.320
So I figure one might as well choose a
therapy first that's cheap, safe, simple,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:34.000
and side effect free, which extended
fasting is most certainly not. But if all

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.940
else fails, it may be worth exploring
under close physician supervision.

